{
  "title": "Home Selling Checklist: Step-by-Step Prep",
  "metaDescription": "Build a customized, printable home selling checklist. Tailor steps for agents or FSBO, vacant or tenant homes, and save or print your worksheet as a PDF.",
  "h1": "Home Selling Checklist",
  "introText": "Getting a house ready to sell runs in seven clear stages: calculating your net proceeds, fixing what buyers and home inspectors will flag, completing state disclosures, staging the property, and managing showings, offers, and closing. At The Modern Wallet, we designed this interactive tool to add the exact steps for your sale type, whether you hire an agent or sell on your own.\n\nUse the tool above to generate your customized checklist. Select your home type, year built, selling method, occupancy status, and property features like pools or septic systems. The tool drops items that do not apply to your home and keeps the tasks you need. Click the \"Print or save as PDF\" button to print a clean worksheet or save it to your computer.",
  "toolHeading": "Build Your Home Selling Checklist",
  "toolSubheading": "Answer a few questions about your property to create your custom preparation checklist.",
  "howItWorks": "The home selling checklist groups your sale into seven distinct stages. Stage one starts with planning the sale, where you establish your timeline and set your budget. Stage two focuses on repairs and physical preparation to address safety concerns before buyers tour. Stage three covers disclosures and legal documentation required by your state. Stage four organizes listing media and showing schedules. Stage five handles offers and contract negotiation. Stage six navigates the escrow period after signing, and stage seven manages the final walk-through and closing.\n\nBefore spending money on preparation, calculate your estimated proceeds and evaluate potential tax obligations. Closing expenses reduce your cash from the sale, so calculate your net figures using the [closing cost calculator](/mortgage/closing-cost-calculator/). Under [IRS Publication 523](https://www.irs.gov/publications/p523), single sellers can exclude up to $250,000 of gain on a primary residence. Married couples filing jointly can exclude up to $500,000 of gain. To qualify for this exclusion, you must have owned and occupied the house as your primary home for two of the five years preceding the sale. If the home was used as an investment property, review [IRS Publication 527](https://www.irs.gov/publications/p527) and calculate taxes using the [capital gains calculator](/real-estate/capital-gains-calculator/).\n\nFor repairs and preparation, focus your budget on functional defects and safety issues that an inspector will note. Major cosmetic upgrades can exceed your budget, so obtain written contractor quotes before starting any large project. Buyers often price visible wear into their purchase offers. You can also hire a licensed inspector for a pre-listing inspection to catch hidden structural or mechanical issues early. If your home uses private waste treatment, the [EPA septic guidance](https://www.epa.gov/septic) notes that septic tanks should be inspected regularly by a professional and pumped as needed.\n\nDisclosures form the legal foundation of your transaction. State law sets required property disclosure forms, which outline known defects with the roof, plumbing, foundation, and systems. If the house was built before 1978, federal law requires sellers to disclose known lead-based paint and provide the official pamphlet per the [EPA real estate disclosure rules](https://www.epa.gov/lead/real-estate-disclosure). Buyers also receive a 10-day window to conduct an independent lead inspection unless they waive it in writing. If the property belongs to a homeowners association, request resale packages and governing documents early because association management companies often take time to process records.\n\nYour selling path shapes the rest of your preparation. Selling by owner requires writing listing descriptions, vetting buyer financing, and handling contract forms directly. If the house sits empty, empty-home insurance endorsements and winter heating precautions help protect the property. For rental sales, [HUD tenant rights resources](https://www.hud.gov/topics/rental_assistance/tenantrights) link to state laws that govern required entry notices and security deposit return rules. Climate hazards also dictate prep: freezing climates require pipe protection, coastal homes need windstorm shutter checks, wildfire regions require brush clearance, and earthquake zones require water heater bracing.\n\nStart your sale preparation by calculating net proceeds and completing safety repairs, then assemble disclosures before marketing the home. If you are coordinating a purchase alongside your sale, follow our [guide to buying and selling a house at the same time](/guides/how-to-buy-and-sell-a-house-at-the-same-time/). Once an offer is accepted, monitor closing tasks and review the buyer inspection items before they complete the [final walkthrough checklist](/checklists/final-walkthrough-checklist/). Complete the questionnaire above, click the \"Print or save as PDF\" button, and begin working through your customized tasks.",
  "workedExample": "In our internal comparison, we ran the checklist's rules for two example homes to see how property traits change the required work. Scenario A evaluated an owner-occupied single-family house built after 2000 in Columbus, Ohio, listed with a real estate agent. The tool kept 30 of the 44 total library items, removing older-home lead disclosures and for-sale-by-owner administrative tasks.\n\nScenario B evaluated a vacant house built in 1965 in Chicago, Illinois, sold by owner with freezing winter conditions active. That scenario kept 33 of 44 library items. The tool removed agent interview steps and occupied-home staging tasks, but added for-sale-by-owner contract management, vacant property security, freezing pipe protection, and federal lead paint disclosures.",
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Spending money on extensive cosmetic remodels without pricing repairs that a licensed home inspector will flag.",
    "Failing to gather homeowners association governing documents, bylaws, and financial statements before accepting an offer.",
    "Setting an asking price from neighboring list prices rather than closed comparable sales.",
    "Leaving prescription medications, personal financial paperwork, and jewelry visible during open houses and scheduled showings.",
    "Wiring closing funds without calling the title company at a known telephone number to confirm wire instructions.",
    "Comparing buyer purchase offers solely on top-line price while overlooking financing terms and inspection contingencies."
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What not to fix before selling your home?",
      "answer": "Avoid spending money on major decorative remodels before getting written quotes, since buyers often price existing condition into their offers. Focus your funds on functional failures, water leaks, and safety hazards that a licensed home inspector will flag. Minor cosmetic imperfections rarely prevent a transaction from closing."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are things to avoid when selling your house?",
      "answer": "Avoid setting your listing price based on neighboring asking prices instead of completed sales. Never hide known defects on state disclosure paperwork or leave valuables exposed during buyer tours. Do not evaluate purchase offers purely on price without reviewing financing terms and closing contingency timelines."
    },
    {
      "question": "What documents do I need to sell my house?",
      "answer": "You need your mortgage payoff statement, property tax records, title ownership documents, and state property disclosure forms. If your home was constructed before 1978, federal lead paint disclosures are mandatory. Homes governed by an association also require covenants, bylaws, and financial reserve packages."
